---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="About Palmistry Path — an independent educational resource on palm reading across Indian, Chinese, and Western traditions."
		/>
		<style>
			main section {
				margin-bottom: 2.5em;
			}
			main h2 {
				font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 3.5vw, 1.5em);
				margin-top: 2em;
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
				padding-bottom: 0.35em;
				border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(201, 169, 110, 0.15);
			}
			main ul {
				padding-left: 1.4em;
				line-height: 1.85;
			}
			main ul li {
				margin-bottom: 0.35em;
				color: var(--color-text-muted);
			}
			.disclaimer {
				margin-top: 3em;
				padding: 1.25em 1.5em;
				border: 1px solid rgba(201, 169, 110, 0.18);
				border-radius: 6px;
				background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
				font-size: 0.84em;
				color: var(--color-text-muted);
				line-height: 1.75;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
			<h1>About</h1>

			<section>
				<h2>What Palmistry Path Is</h2>
				<p>
					Palmistry Path is an independent educational resource dedicated to palmistry as a structured
					practice and area of study. The goal is a well-organised, honest guide to the subject — one
					grounded in the primary traditions (Indian, Chinese, and Western palmistry) rather than in
					pop-culture summaries or unfounded claims.
				</p>
				<p>
					Content is organised as a progressive learning path: from foundational concepts through
					individual lines, mounts, and advanced interpretation. No prior knowledge is assumed.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Editorial Philosophy</h2>
				<p>Every article on this site aims to:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						Attribute meanings to specific traditions rather than presenting any single view as
						universal
					</li>
					<li>
						Note clearly when traditions disagree, rather than synthesising a false consensus
					</li>
					<li>
						Use language that reflects the interpretive, observational nature of palmistry:
						"traditionally associated with…", "often interpreted as…", "may suggest…"
					</li>
					<li>Acknowledge the cultural and historical context of each tradition</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Sources</h2>
				<p>
					Interpretive material on this site rests mainly on five works, which recur across the
					lessons and articles:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Cheiro (William John Warner), <em>Palmistry for All</em> (1916)</li>
					<li>William G. Benham, <em>The Laws of Scientific Hand Reading</em> (1900)</li>
					<li>Fred Gettings, <em>The Book of the Hand</em> (1965)</li>
					<li>Peter West, <em>The Complete Illustrated Guide to Palmistry</em> (1998)</li>
					<li>Johnny Fincham, <em>The Spellbinding Power of Palmistry</em> (2005)</li>
				</ul>
				<p>Individual articles also draw on:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Cheiro, <em>Cheiro's Language of the Hand</em> (1897)</li>
					<li>Stanislas d'Arpentigny, <em>La Chirognomonie</em> (1843)</li>
					<li>Comte de Saint-Germain, <em>The Practice of Palmistry</em> (1897)</li>
					<li>Andrew Fitzherbert, <em>Hand Psychology</em> (1986)</li>
					<li>Ellen Goldberg &amp; Doris Dobkins, <em>The Art and Science of Hand Reading</em> (2016)</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Where an article covers the Indian or Chinese traditions, or makes a historical,
					anatomical, or scientific claim, it cites sources appropriate to that material rather than
					the Western palmistry canon — scholarly editions such as Kenneth Zysk's
					<em>The Indian System of Human Marks</em> (2016), classical texts such as Varāhamihira's
					<em>Bṛhat Saṃhitā</em>, academic work on Chinese physiognomy, and peer-reviewed or
					reference literature for factual claims. Each article lists what it used.
				</p>
				<p>
					Where sources disagree, both views are presented rather than reconciled. Commercial
					astrology sites, content farms, and unsourced blogs are not used as sources.
				</p>
				<p>
					A note on where this stands: the site is working through a review of its own citations,
					and some articles are further along than others. Where a claim reflects contemporary
					practice rather than something traceable to a classical text, the aim is to say so on the
					page rather than let the bibliography imply more than it can support.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What This Site Does Not Claim</h2>
				<p>
					Palmistry Path is not a fortune-telling service. No content here claims to predict future
					events, and palmistry is not presented as a science with verified predictive accuracy.
				</p>
				<p>
					Nothing here should be taken as medical, psychological, legal, or financial advice.
					Palmistry is treated as a centuries-old interpretive tradition — one worth studying
					carefully, honestly, and with intellectual curiosity.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Corrections</h2>
				<p>
					If you find an error, a misattribution, or a claim that doesn't hold up to scrutiny,
					corrections are welcome. This site is built to improve over time.
				</p>
				<p>
					For now, corrections can be sent through the contact address that will be published here
					shortly.
				</p>
			</section>

			<aside class="disclaimer">
				<em>Palmistry Path is for education, personal reflection, and exploration of a
				centuries-old interpretive tradition. It is not a substitute for professional medical,
				legal, or financial advice.</em>
			</aside>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
